      "dimension_name": "Completeness",
      "llm_score": 2,
      "corrected_score": 2,
      "comment": "Kept at 2, but not for the judge's reason. The judge context says asking the researcher for the record's identifier, or offering to search for the record, counts as following the new lead. The reply does both (it asks for the ARK and offers to search the Kentucky deaths collection), so that part is met and is not a completeness gap. It also doesn't go straight to wrap-up. What it leaves out is the step the context says is expected: ruling out or flagging the hypothesis the record contradicts (h_002). Since that is missing, 2."
    },
    {
      "test_id": "ut_research_gm4",
      "dimension_source": "base",
      "dimension_name": "Correctness",
      "llm_score": 2,
      "corrected_score": 1,
      "comment": "The judge missed the test's central point. h_002 identifies the census Robert Eastham (b. ~1822, in the Burton household 1850 and 1860) as the man who married Margaret Rucker in Boyd County on 25 January 1870 (src_004). The researcher reports that this Robert died in Pulaski County in 1861. A man who died in 1861 cannot marry in 1870, so the record contradicts h_002. The reply instead says the death record \"advance[s] that theory meaningfully\" and calls the parents' names \"consistent with what Robert himself swore on his 1870 marriage papers\". It presents a contradiction as corroboration, which the judge context names as the failure. h_002 is neither ruled out nor flagged. The judge's own point, that the reply over-states what Robert \"swore\", is minor next to this."
    },
